# Lesson 6, batch 2 (2026-11-28): partner overcalled; lead his suit against 3NT, not your own weak spades.
title: Partner came in with hearts
id: L06-09
type: exercise
lesson: 6
concepts: 6.2, 6.1
student: S
board: 9

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  96     KQJ32  JT7     AT2
  E  KJ8    A76    92      KQ983
  S  QT742  95     8653    J4
  W  A53    T84    AKQ4    765

auction:
  N Pass   = Not enough to open
  E 1C     = 13+ points, 4+ clubs
  S Pass   = Not enough to bid
  W 1D     = 6+ points, 4+ diamonds
  N 1H     = A good heart suit. Partner wants you to know about it.
  E 1NT    = A balanced minimum, about 13-15, with hearts stopped
  S Pass   = Nothing to add
  W 3NT    = Enough for game opposite a minimum
  N Pass   = Nothing more to say
  E Pass   = Happy with game
  S Pass   = Nothing to add

auction recap:
  East opened 1♣ and West responded 1♦. Partner (North) passed at first, but
  then came in with 1♥ to tell you about his hearts. East's 1NT showed a
  balanced minimum with a heart stopper, and West raised to game. East is
  declarer, and so you, South, will lead to the first trick.

lead planning:
  declarer: 13-15 = East opened, then rebid 1NT: a balanced minimum, about 13 to 15.
  dummy: 11-15 = West responded, then jumped to game opposite a minimum: about 12 or more.
  partner: 7-12 = 40, minus your 3, East's 13 to 15 and West's 11 to 15, leaves 7 to 13. North passed at first, so 12 at most.
  remind: Partner bid hearts after passing. What is he telling you?
  remind: Your spades are long, but where are the high cards to make them good?

play:
  1   S H9*  W H4  N HJ  E H6
      H9 key: Partner's suit. With two small cards, lead the higher one: the 9.
      H9 not H5: Right suit! From two small cards, lead the higher one first, so partner can tell you are short.
      H9 not S: Your spades are long, but with only the queen and ten on top they take a long time to set up, and you have no way back in. Which suit did partner bid?
      H9 not D: West bid diamonds. Which suit did partner bid?
      H9 not C: East opened clubs. Partner told you which suit he likes.
      note: Partner plays the jack, and declarer lets it win, holding on to her ace.
  2   N HK  E HA  S H5  W H8
      note: Partner leads the king, and this time declarer takes her ace.
  3   E CK  S C4  W C5  N CA
      note: Declarer needs club tricks and leads the king. Partner wins the ♣A at once: his hearts are ready.
  4   N HQ  E H7  S S2  W HT
      note: Partner's ♥Q drops dummy's 10. You have no hearts left, so you throw a small spade.
  5   N H3  E S8  S S4  W S3
  6   N H2  E D2  S S7  W S5
      note: Five tricks for the defense: 3NT is down one.
  7   N DJ  E D9  S D3  W DQ
  8   W C6  N C2  E CQ  S CJ
  9   E C9  S D5  W C7  N CT
      note: Partner's ♣10 takes another trick: down two.
  10  N S6  E SJ  S SQ  W SA
  11  W DA  N D7  E C3  S D6
  12  W DK  N DT  E C8  S D8
  13  W D4  N S9  E SK  S ST

play recap:
  Your ♥9 went straight to partner's suit. Declarer held up her ace once,
  but partner still had the ♣A to get back in, and his hearts ran: four
  heart tricks and the ♣A beat 3NT, and the ♣10 made it down two. A spade
  lead would have given declarer time to set up the clubs, and 3NT would
  have made with an overtrick or two. When partner bids, his suit is usually
  the lead that counts, even when you have a long suit of your own.
